            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we found some substantial air leakage in the following locations:



** The location of the chimney that goes up from the basement through the main floor &amp; into the attic.



** The 6" combustion pipe for the current furnace &amp; pellet stove (can be eliminated in conjunction with the upgrades recommended).



** The older doors &amp; windows.



** The basement wall that adjoins the space below the garage has alot of leakage



** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls.



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you can achieve about a 50% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 531 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.2 GJ, and qualify for a $1,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 R12 and have an exterior layer of semi-rigid fibreglass insulation (about R6) beneath the wood siding. We discussed your interest to add more insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For your house, I modeled adding R15 to the exterior of your walls.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 358 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), This rebate is for increasing 100% of the exterior walls, including the wall between the main floor that adjoins the garage.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

There are left &amp; right basement sections to your house. The left side is concrete block with 0.5" (R2.5) of rigid insulation on the interior, plus some rigid insulation on the exterior of these walls that appears to continue below grade. The right section of basement has a lower shallow level that is all concrete block and an upper level that has concrete block walls with an upper pony wall. The concrete block is mostly uninsulated &amp; the pony wall is constructed the same as the rest of the upper level walls.



I have included the pony walls with the rest of the upper walls for an insulation upgrade recommendation. For all of the concrete block sections of the basement, I am recommending you add a minimum R10 to the interior.



Savings: By adding a minimum R10 to100% of the interior of your concrete block foundation walls, you could potentially save $ 405 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.1 GJ, and you would qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</foundation>
                <windows>

Your current windows are older wood ones that are in need of an upgrade. In conjunction with your planned exterior wall insulation upgrade, this will represent a good time (as you could qualify for the $250 per window rebate) to update the 11 older wood windows to triple pane.



I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the 11 current windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these 11 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 434 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17.3 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 182 litre electric hot water tank. For you future planning, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92 or higher, could potentially save $ 1,113 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older propane furnace (80% efficiency) and you have a supplemental wood pellet stove in the basement. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (more insulation &amp; less air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 788 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 26.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Pellet Stove Supplemental Heat



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency furnace &amp; the cost of wood pellets purchased) if you were to use the wood pellet stove in the basement for about 50% of your heating requirements.



Savings: By using your wood pellet stove for 50% of the heating requirements of your home, you could potentially save $ 243 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane furnace.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has very little mechanical ventilation:

        - the kitchen range hood fan recirculates inside the house

        - the one bathroom fan is broken

        - there is a clothes dryer that exhausts to the exterior



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (just the dryer vents to the outside), indicated a pressure drop of 0.2 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not estimated to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current propane furnace and the pellet stove are both power vented and less susceptible to back drafting. There is a 6" combustion air supply pipe that comes into the basement to supply combustion air for both of these appliances. With the extensive renovations planned for this house (insulation, air sealing, new heating appliances, more spot ventilation etc.) this house will see more "depressurization" however this will be OK as your new appliances will be sealed combustion. If you also install a dedicated combustion air pipe sealed directly to the pellet stove, you can eliminate the 6" open combustion air pipe (reducing unnecessary air leakage even further).



During my assessment of your home, there were no functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
